      <header class="cod-title-block">
        <p class="cod-chapter-label">Chapter Eight · Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits?</p>
        <h1 class="cod-title">The Tithe</h1>
        <p class="cod-subtitle">Into Whose Storehouse?</p>
        <p class="cod-tagline">Yahuah commanded a tithe. The church calls itself the storehouse and the pastor the priest. Scripture says the temple and the priest are you.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="cod-pullquote">Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? Let Scripture and history decide.</p>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">INTRODUCTION</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Question No One Is Asking</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The plates are about to be passed. Before they go out, someone reads Malachi 3: "Will a man rob God? Yet ye have robbed me … In tithes and offerings. Ye are cursed with a curse." Then the promise: "Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse … and prove me now herewith … if I will not open you the windows of heaven." The band starts to play, soft and slow, as the ushers move down the rows. Ten percent of the paycheck goes in, before taxes. At the end of the year a giving statement comes in the mail, ready for the tax return.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Behind that moment sit three beliefs almost no one questions. <strong>The church is the storehouse.</strong> <strong>The pastor is the priest</strong>, the Levite of the congregation, who lives from the tithe. <strong>And the building is the house of God</strong>, which the tithe and the building fund must keep up.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The tithe is in the Law of Yahuah (Leviticus 27:30), and Yahushua upheld it: "these ought ye to have done" (Matthew 23:23). But the tithe was built around three things — a temple, a priesthood, and the outsiders it fed. This chapter asks three questions all the way through: <strong>Who is the temple? Who is the priest? Who are the outsiders?</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-body">The same churches that teach the Law was nailed to the cross — no Sabbath, no feasts, no clean and unclean — have kept one commandment out of it, and they keep it every week. They read Malachi 3 over the plate and stop before Malachi 4, where Yahuah says, "Remember ye the law of Moses my servant" (Malachi 4:4). <strong>Out of the whole Law, the one command the church kept is the one that pays the church.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture names the power behind a practice like that: "some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits" (1 Timothy 4:1). Against it stands the Spirit of truth, who "will guide you into all truth" (John 16:13). In the altar call, the enemy erased the blood. <strong>In the tithe, he moved the storehouse</strong> — from the believer's own gate, where the widow ate, to the church's account.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">I</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Tithe Yahuah Gave</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>What it was.</strong> The increase of the land and the flock: "the seed of the land, or … the fruit of the tree," and "the tithe of the herd, or of the flock" (Leviticus 27:30, 32). Grain, wine, oil, and animals.</li>
        <li><strong>The place.</strong> The tithe was brought to "the place which Yahuah shall choose" (Deuteronomy 14:23), and the storehouse was the set of temple chambers where the grain, wine, and oil were kept (Nehemiah 13:5, 12) — "that there may be meat in mine house" (Malachi 3:10). Food, not a building fund.</li>
        <li><strong>The priesthood.</strong> The tribe of Levi had no land. "I have given the children of Levi all the tenth in Israel for an inheritance, for their service" (Numbers 18:21). The Levites gave a tenth of that to the priests (Numbers 18:26).</li>
        <li><strong>The giver himself.</strong> The tithe was eaten at the feast: "thou shalt rejoice, thou, and thine household, and the Levite that is within thy gates" (Deuteronomy 14:26-27).</li>
        <li><strong>The outsiders.</strong> Every third year the tithe was laid up in the towns, and "the stranger, and the fatherless, and the widow … shall come, and shall eat and be satisfied" (Deuteronomy 14:28-29).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">"But We Don't Farm Anymore"</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">The usual answer is that Israel was a farming nation, so the tithe was paid in food, and today it is paid in money. But Israel was never without money. Abraham paid "four hundred shekels of silver, current money with the merchant" for a field (Genesis 23:16). Israel had craftsmen, smiths, and builders (Exodus 31:1-6). Yahushua was a carpenter, Peter a fisherman, Paul a tentmaker, and Matthew a tax collector. Not one of them is ever told to tithe his wages.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The Law even planned for money. If the journey was too far to carry the tithe, "then shalt thou turn it into money" — and when the family arrived, "thou shalt bestow that money for whatsoever thy soul lusteth after, for oxen, or for sheep, or for wine," and eat it before Yahuah with the household and the Levite (Deuteronomy 14:25-27). <strong>When the tithe became money, the Law turned it back into a meal.</strong> And if the tithe can be updated because times have changed, so could the Sabbath and the feasts. <strong>The church updates the Law where updating pays, and cancels it where keeping it would cost.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-body">Malachi fits inside that picture. The book is aimed at the priests (Malachi 1:6; 2:1), and five verses before "Will a man rob God?" Yahuah names whom He will judge: those who "oppress the hireling in his wages, the widow, and the fatherless, and that turn aside the stranger" (Malachi 3:5). This is the tithe of the Spirit of truth: a feast before Yahuah, the Levite at the table, and the widow fed.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">II</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Through New Testament Eyes</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Who Is the Temple?</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">"Know ye not that ye are the temple of God, and that the Spirit of God dwelleth in you?" (1 Corinthians 3:16). Believers are "builded together for an habitation of God through the Spirit" (Ephesians 2:22). Stephen said it to the men who owned the most famous building on earth: "the most High dwelleth not in temples made with hands" (Acts 7:48). <strong>The house of Yahuah is not on the corner of Main Street. It is His people.</strong></p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Who Is the Priest?</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Peter wrote to ordinary believers: "ye are … a royal priesthood" (1 Peter 2:9). Over all of them stands one High Priest, Yahushua, "after the order of Melchisedec" (Hebrews 6:20) — and Hebrews ties the tithe to that order: "here men that die receive tithes; but there he receiveth them, of whom it is witnessed that he liveth" (Hebrews 7:8). <strong>No pastor is a Levite, and no pastor is Melchizedek.</strong></p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">"They Which Preach the Gospel Should Live of the Gospel"</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul did write, "the Master ordained that they which preach the gospel should live of the gospel" (1 Corinthians 9:14). In context it is a right to food and lodging for men on the road, not a tenth of anyone's wages — and Paul refused it and told the believers to follow his example. His last word to the elders of Ephesus was that they should work with their own hands "to support the weak" (Acts 20:35). The full answer is in the Go Deeper panel below.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Who Are the Outsiders?</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">The stranger, the fatherless, and the widow did not disappear at the cross. "I was a stranger, and ye took me in … Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me" (Matthew 25:35, 40). James called it pure religion "to visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction" (James 1:27). The apostles asked Paul for one thing: "that we should remember the poor" (Galatians 2:10). This is the order the Spirit of truth keeps: the believer is the house, the believer is the priest, and the poor are fed.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The temple is the believer. The priest is the believer, under one High Priest who lives. The outsiders are still at the gate. Every part of the history that follows moved the tithe away from all three.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Paul and the Right He Refused</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">"Even so hath the Master ordained that they which preach the gospel should live of the gospel" (1 Corinthians 9:14). "Let the elders that rule well be counted worthy of double honour, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine" (1 Timothy 5:17). These are the verses every pastor stands on. Read where they stand, five things come out of the text.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>It is a right, not a tithe.</strong> Paul argues from a soldier, a vineyard, a flock, and an ox that is not muzzled (1 Corinthians 9:7-9). He never mentions a tenth, never sets a percentage, never ties it to a building, and never threatens a curse.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>It covered food and lodging for men on the road.</strong> Paul is defending the right of traveling apostles — "the other apostles, and … the brethren of the Master, and Cephas" — to "eat and to drink" and to bring a wife on the journey (9:4-5). It is the right Yahushua gave the men He sent out two by two: "in the same house remain, eating and drinking such things as they give: for the labourer is worthy of his hire" (Luke 10:7). He told them, "Provide neither gold, nor silver, nor brass in your purses … for the workman is worthy of his meat" (Matthew 10:9-10). His meat — his food. Not a salary, a house, and a pension. Even the altar comparison is food: "they which wait at the altar are partakers with the altar" (1 Corinthians 9:13). The priests ate a share of the table.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Paul refused it, and told the believers to copy him.</strong> "We have not used this power" (9:12). "It were better for me to die, than that any man should make my glorying void" (9:15). To the Thessalonians: "Neither did we eat any man's bread for nought; but wrought with labour and travail night and day … Not because we have not power, but to make ourselves an ensample unto you to follow us" (2 Thessalonians 3:8-9). The right is in the text. So is the example, and the example is what he told them to follow.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>He said it straight to the shepherds.</strong> To the elders of Ephesus, the men he told to "feed the church of God" (Acts 20:28), his last words were these: "I have coveted no man's silver, or gold … these hands have ministered unto my necessities … so labouring ye ought to support the weak, and to remember the words of the Master Yahushua, how he said, It is more blessed to give than to receive" (Acts 20:33-35). The shepherds were to work and support the weak, not be supported by them.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Scripture warns of the reverse: "Woe be to the shepherds of Israel that do feed themselves! … Ye eat the fat, and ye clothe you with the wool … but ye feed not the flock" (Ezekiel 34:2-3). "The priests thereof teach for hire, and the prophets thereof divine for money" (Micah 3:11). Peter told the elders to feed the flock "not for filthy lucre" (1 Peter 5:2).</p>
          <p class="cod-body">A believer may gladly share his table with a man who teaches him the Word (Galatians 6:6), and the assembly may help an elder who labors hard in it. That is a gift from a willing heart. Nothing in the text makes the teacher a Levite, makes the tithe his salary, or lets him leave his work and live off the flock.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">III</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Priests Again (A.D. 100-400)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">A church manual from around A.D. 100, the <em>Didache</em>, told believers to give the firstfruits of their wine, grain, and flocks to their prophets — "for they are your high priests." About A.D. 255, Cyprian, bishop of Carthage, pointed to the Levites, who "received tithes from the eleven tribes for their sustenance," and wrote, "The same principle holds now for the clergy." In 313 Constantine freed the clergy from public duties, in 321 made it lawful for churches to receive inheritances, and paid for the first great basilicas. The church now had priests, buildings, and an income.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture records a king who did the same. Jeroboam feared the people would take their offerings to Jerusalem, so he built his own houses of worship at Bethel and Dan, "and made priests of the lowest of the people, which were not of the sons of Levi" (1 Kings 12:31). "This thing became a sin" (12:30). <strong>A house built by men, a priesthood chosen by men, and the people's offerings kept from where Yahuah sent them — that is a seducing spirit's pattern.</strong> The believer is the priest (1 Peter 2:9), and his High Priest lives (Hebrews 7:24-25). The church did not need to deny it. It only needed to hire a priest.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">IV</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Tithe Becomes Law (A.D. 494-1977)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Around 494, Pope Gelasius divided church income into four parts: the bishop, the clergy, the buildings, and the poor. The poor were now one share out of four. Then church councils ordered the tithe, Charlemagne made it the law of his empire, and the Protestant churches kept it — collecting it from every landholder and jailing those who refused. England did not end the last payments until 1977.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahuah asked for offerings from the willing heart: "of every man that giveth it willingly with his heart ye shall take my offering" (Exodus 25:2). Scripture says who takes a tenth by force. When Israel demanded a king, Samuel warned, "He will take the tenth of your seed … He will take the tenth of your sheep: and ye shall be his servants" (1 Samuel 8:15, 17) — and Yahuah said of that demand, "they have rejected me" (8:7). <strong>A tenth taken by force is the mark of the king men set up in Yahuah's place.</strong> When the church took the tithe by threat of excommunication, fines, and jail, it was collecting the king's tenth, not Yahuah's.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Tithe Barns, Fines, and the Tithe War</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The councils.</strong> In 567 the Council of Tours urged believers to pay the tithe. In 585 the Council of Mâcon ordered it, and those who refused were to be excommunicated. Around 779 Charlemagne made the tithe the law of his empire, enforced by the state.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The barns.</strong> Across Europe the church built great tithe barns to hold the tenth of every harvest — long stone buildings, some as large as a church, many still standing in England and France. The storehouse had become literal again, but it belonged to the church and the manor, not to the widow and the stranger.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The Protestant tithe.</strong> The Reformation kept the tithe. In England the established church collected a tenth from every landholder in the parish, whatever church he attended. Quakers refused on conscience, and were fined, had their goods seized, and were jailed. In 1753 Joseph Besse published two thick volumes recording these "sufferings," most of them over tithes.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The Tithe War.</strong> In Ireland, Catholic farmers were made to pay tithes to the Protestant Church of Ireland, a church most of them never attended. From 1831 they refused in large numbers, and the government sent police and soldiers to seize cattle and crops. In December 1831 at Carrickshock, in County Kilkenny, a crowd killed more than a dozen police escorting a tithe collector. The resistance ran until 1836, and in 1838 the tithe was folded into rent paid by landlords.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The long end.</strong> In 1836 England replaced the tithe in kind with a money charge on land, the tithe rentcharge. The Tithe Act of 1936 abolished the rentcharge but required landowners to pay it off through annuities, and the last of those payments did not end until 1977.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Yahushua told Peter that the children of the kingdom are free from the tribute kings take: "Of whom do the kings of the earth take custom or tribute? of their own children, or of strangers? … Then are the children free" (Matthew 17:25-26).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">V</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Storehouse Comes to America (1600s-1920s)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Most American colonies taxed their people to support an official church; Massachusetts was the last state to stop, in 1833. After that, American churches had to pay their own way, and many rented pews, with the best seats going to the families who paid the most. Then a movement brought the tithe back, and with it a new teaching often called <strong>storehouse tithing</strong>: the local church is the storehouse of Malachi 3, the full tenth belongs to it first, and gifts to a neighbor in need or a widow down the street come only after the church is paid.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The church also gave the words its own meanings. Many say the tithe is for the church, and the "offerings" above it are for the widows and the needy. Scripture has it the other way. <strong>The tithe itself was the part that fed the widow</strong> — "the stranger, and the fatherless, and the widow … shall come, and shall eat and be satisfied" (Deuteronomy 14:29; 26:12). Freewill offerings were gifts over and above (Deuteronomy 16:10). The church kept the tithe and left the widow the leftovers.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahushua warned against the scribes "which devour widows' houses" (Mark 12:40). Then He watched a poor widow drop into the temple treasury "all that she had, even all her living" (Mark 12:44), and the next thing He did was walk out and say that not one stone of that building would be left standing (Mark 13:1-2). <strong>The Law built a tithe to feed the widow. The system He walked out of taught the widow to feed it.</strong> He named the power behind it: "Ye cannot serve God and mammon" — and the Pharisees, "who were covetous," derided Him (Luke 16:13-14). Mammon is not a coin. It is a master, and a seducing spirit serves it.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Thomas Kane and the Storehouse</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>After the state churches.</strong> When the state stopped paying for churches, American congregations tried many ways to raise money: pew rents, subscription lists, church fairs, and suppers. Pew renting was common through much of the 1800s. The best pews near the front went to the families who paid the most, and the poor sat in the back or in the gallery.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The Layman.</strong> In 1876 a Chicago businessman named Thomas Kane, a Presbyterian, began printing short tithing pamphlets and mailing them free to pastors, signing himself simply "Layman." He gathered testimonies from men who said they had prospered after they began to tithe. By 1903 he wrote that pastors had ordered his tithing literature "in quantities aggregating many millions."</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The young people.</strong> The Christian Endeavor youth movement, founded in 1881, took up the cause with a pledge called the "Tenth Legion": a promise to give a tenth of one's income to the work of the church. More than 25,000 young people signed it. Denominations formed stewardship committees, held tithing campaigns, and printed tithing pledge cards.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The storehouse.</strong> Out of this came the teaching that the local church is Malachi's storehouse and that the whole tenth must go there before any other gift. Some denominations wrote it into their stewardship programs in the early 1900s, and it became the standard teaching in many American churches. The tithe had moved from the Levite's table and the widow's gate to the church treasurer's office.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Paul's collection went the other way. He asked the assemblies to set money aside "as God hath prospered him" (1 Corinthians 16:2), and every coin of it went to the poor saints at Jerusalem (Romans 15:26).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VI</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Caesar's Receipt (1913-Today)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">In 1913 the United States began taxing income, and from 1917 taxpayers could deduct gifts to churches. In 1954 churches were placed in section <strong>501(c)(3)</strong> of the tax code, and in the same law the Johnson Amendment barred them from supporting or opposing candidates for office. The pulpit now had a rule set by Washington. Since 1993 a gift of $250 or more needs a written receipt before it can be deducted — which is why the giving statement arrives every January.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahushua taught that giving belongs in secret: "let not thy left hand know what thy right hand doeth: That thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly" (Matthew 6:3-4). Of those who give to be seen, He said, "They have their reward" (Matthew 6:2). Scripture shows what happens when Yahuah's house reshapes itself to please the empire: King Ahaz copied the altar of Damascus, rebuilt Yahuah's house to match it, and changed its arrangements "for the king of Assyria" (2 Kings 16:10-18). <strong>The church gained a tax break and accepted Caesar's rule over its pulpit. That trade is a seducing spirit's work.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Caesar's Receipt</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The income tax.</strong> The Sixteenth Amendment was ratified in 1913, and the United States began taxing income. Churches had always been free of such taxes. In 1917, to keep giving up during the First World War, Congress let taxpayers deduct gifts to churches and charities. From that year on, the tithe could lower a man's taxes. In 1921 Congress stopped taxing the value of a parsonage provided to a minister.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>501(c)(3).</strong> In 1954 Congress rewrote the whole tax code, and churches were placed in section 501(c)(3), along with charities and schools. In the same law, Senator Lyndon Johnson added the rule now called the Johnson Amendment: an organization in 501(c)(3) may not support or oppose any candidate for office. The 1954 code also let ministers receive a cash housing allowance free of income tax.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The corporation.</strong> Churches are treated as 501(c)(3) automatically, but most incorporate under state law as nonprofit corporations, and many apply for an official letter from the Internal Revenue Service. A corporation is a legal body created by a charter from the state. It can own property, sign contracts, borrow money, and be sued, apart from the people in it, and it answers to the state that created it.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Two bodies.</strong> When a congregation incorporates, it has two bodies. One is the assembly, which Scripture calls "the body of Messiah" (1 Corinthians 12:27). The other is a legal body chartered by the government, which holds the bank account, the deed, and the receipts. Many believers never know which body their tithe goes to. It goes to the second one.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The receipt.</strong> Since 1993, any single gift of $250 or more must have a written acknowledgment from the church before the giver can deduct it. The gift Yahushua said should be in secret is now recorded, receipted, and reported to the government.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VII</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Building Fund</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Most churches carry a building fund, and many run capital campaigns that ask for pledges over three to five years, on top of the tithe. Some keep a building fund open for decades without ever buying a building; others now meet in buildings worth millions. The building the members paid for is usually owned by the church corporation, governed by a board that in some churches the pastor and his family control. In many denominations it is not even the local church's. The United Methodist Church has held every congregation's property "in trust" for the denomination since Wesley's day; when more than 7,600 congregations left between 2019 and 2023, they had to meet financial conditions and win a two-thirds vote to keep the buildings they had paid for. In the Seventh-day Adventist Church, the title to local church property is held by the regional conference. The members give. Someone else holds the deed.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">"Howbeit the most High dwelleth not in temples made with hands" (Acts 7:48); He "is not worshipped with men's hands, as though he needed any thing" (Acts 17:25). The first great building project in Scripture was Babel: "let us build us a city and a tower … and let us make us a name" (Genesis 11:4). <strong>A building fund that never builds, and a building the givers do not own, are a seducing spirit's tools: they keep the people's eyes on a structure and their offerings flowing toward it, and away from the temple Yahuah lives in.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VIII</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Seed Faith (1950s-Today)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">In the 1950s the healing evangelist Oral Roberts taught supporters to give to his ministry as a "seed" and to expect God to multiply it back. By 1970 he had a book called <em>Miracle of Seed-Faith</em>. In 1987 he told supporters that God would "call him home" unless they sent eight million dollars. The idea spread across television ministries and into ordinary churches: "sow a seed," and Malachi 3:10 becomes a money-back guarantee.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">"Freely ye have received, freely give" (Matthew 10:8). When Simon offered money for the gift of the Spirit, Peter said, "Thy money perish with thee" (Acts 8:20). Elisha took nothing from Naaman, but Gehazi ran after him and took the silver, and the leprosy clung to him (2 Kings 5:16, 20-27). Peter warned of teachers who "through covetousness … with feigned words make merchandise of you," following "the way of Balaam … who loved the wages of unrighteousness" (2 Peter 2:3, 15). The bargain itself is the tempter's: "All these things will I give thee, if thou wilt fall down and worship me" (Matthew 4:9). <strong>Give me something, and I will give you more. That is not the Father's voice; it is a seducing spirit's.</strong> When the threat of a curse is used to pry the money loose, the voice is the accuser's (Revelation 12:10).</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Scripture Shows Instead</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The tithe was never cancelled (Matthew 23:23). Under the Spirit of truth, read through New Testament eyes, it looks like this:</p>

      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>The temple is the believer's house and body.</strong> Yahuah's tenth is set apart there, by the priest who lives there (1 Corinthians 3:16; 1 Peter 2:9).</li>
        <li><strong>The feast is still kept.</strong> The tithe eaten "before Yahuah" with joy is still for keeping His appointed times with the household, and for bringing "the Levite, the stranger, and the fatherless, and the widow" to the table (Deuteronomy 16:11, 14).</li>
        <li><strong>The outsiders are fed.</strong> The widow, the orphan, and the stranger at the believer's own gate are the storehouse's first purpose (Deuteronomy 14:28-29; James 1:27).</li>
        <li><strong>Those who labor in the Word may be supported</strong> — freely, from a willing heart (Galatians 6:6; 2 Corinthians 9:7) — not as a tax, not with a curse, and not for a building.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="cod-body">That is a tithe the widow eats from, not one she pays into.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Is the Harm?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul said the fight is "against principalities, against powers" (Ephesians 6:12), and Yahushua named one of them. Mammon is the only power He ever set beside the Father as a rival master (Matthew 6:24). "The love of money is the root of all evil," and some who coveted it "erred from the faith" (1 Timothy 6:10).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The church's tithe taught the believer that he is not the temple, so he looks for Yahuah in a building. It taught him he is not a priest, so he pays another man to be one. It taught him his duty is paid in full once the envelope is in the plate, while the widow and the fatherless in his own town are left to someone else. A seducing spirit does not have to stop the giving. It only has to aim it.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The offering comes with music, as the altar call does in Chapter Six — a soft song so the hand reaches for the wallet while the heart is stirred. Chapter Two, <em>Music: The Sound of Self-Worship</em>, traced the pattern to the plain of Dura, where the music was the signal to bow (Daniel 3:5): "The music did not express the worship — it caused it." At the altar call the music moves people to walk forward. At the offering it moves them to give. Amos saw the same scene in Israel: "Though ye offer me burnt offerings … I will not accept them … Take thou away from me the noise of thy songs" (Amos 5:22-23), because "they turn aside the poor in the gate" (5:12). <strong>Offerings with music, while the poor wait at the gate — Yahuah called it noise.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>Mammon does not need to steal the tithe. He only needs to change the address on the envelope.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Verdict</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The church's tithe is not a doctrine anyone fights over. It is a practice, and so few ever check it against the Law it claims to come from. The record runs in one direction: a manual that called the prophets "your high priests," a bishop who said the clergy are the Levites, an emperor who gave the church an income, councils and kings who made the tithe a tax, Protestant churches that jailed men for not paying it, a businessman's pamphlets, a storehouse teaching, a receipt from Caesar, a building fund, and a seed-faith promise. Every step moved the tithe further from the temple Yahuah lives in, the priests He made, and the widow at the gate. That is the direction a seducing spirit moves.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">It is the only piece of the Law most of these churches kept. "Ye pay tithe of mint and anise and cummin, and have omitted the weightier matters of the law, judgment, mercy, and faith" (Matthew 23:23). When a church throws out the Sabbath, the feasts, and the commandments, and keeps only the tenth, the question is no longer what Yahuah said. It is who benefits. In the altar call, the enemy erased the blood. In the tithe, he moved the storehouse.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">To Laodicea, Yahushua said, "Because thou sayest, I am rich, and increased with goods, and have need of nothing; and knowest not that thou art wretched, and miserable, and poor, and blind, and naked: I counsel thee to buy of me gold tried in the fire" (Revelation 3:17-18). Over the city whose merchants traded in "slaves, and souls of men" (Revelation 18:13), the voice from heaven still calls: "Come out of her, my people" (Revelation 18:4).</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The believer is the temple. The believer is the priest. The widow is at the gate. That is the storehouse Yahuah meant.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-end"><span class="cod-end-mark">◆</span></div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

